course: religion-ethics
lesson: 64
title: Days of remembering: sorokovyny, memorial days, Remembrance Day and the poppy
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The funeral is not the last goodbye: many families and whole countries keep special days to remember the dead again, because love does not stop when someone dies, and remembering together helps people heal.
explain: Picture Taras's family forty days after his grandmother died: they go to church for prayers, then share a meal at home with kutia, a dish of wheat, honey and poppy seeds; this is sorokovyny, from the Ukrainian word for forty.
explain: In November Ellie's class makes paper poppies, and at 11 o'clock on 11 November the whole school stands still for two minutes, remembering the soldiers who died in wars, and also the nurses, civilians and children; poppies grew on the battlefields of the First World War, so they became the sign of remembering.
explain: Other days of remembering: Anzac Day dawn services in Australia and New Zealand, All Souls' Day on 2 November when Catholic churches read out names, Provody in Ukraine when families bring Easter eggs to graves, and the yearly anniversary when Ellie's mum lights a candle for Granny.
explain: Faiths remember in different ways: Orthodox and Catholic Christians pray for the dead, other Christians remember them without praying for them in the same way, Jews light a yahrzeit candle each year, and people of every belief keep silence and work for peace.
fact: Sorokovyny is a Ukrainian memorial on the 40th day after a death; Orthodox and Greek Catholic Christians also hold memorial prayers on the 9th day and after a year.
fact: Many Orthodox Christians believe the soul is on a journey to God during the forty days after death.
fact: Kutia is a Ukrainian dish of wheat, honey and poppy seeds, eaten at memorials and on Christmas Eve; the wheat is a sign of new life and resurrection.
fact: Red poppies grew on the battlefields of the First World War; the poem «In Flanders Fields» by John McCrae helped make them a symbol of remembrance.
fact: The First World War ended with an armistice at 11 o'clock on 11 November 1918; many countries keep two minutes' silence at that time each year.
fact: War memorials list the names of local people who died in wars; sometimes several men from one family are listed.
fact: Anzac Day, on 25 April, remembers Australians and New Zealanders who served and died in wars; dawn services recall the landing at Gallipoli at dawn in 1915.
fact: The anniversary of a death can bring back strong feelings; many people light a candle or look at photos on that day.
fact: Jews light a yahrzeit candle on the anniversary of a death; Catholics may have a Mass said for the person.
fact: All Souls' Day, 2 November, is when Catholics and some Anglicans pray for the dead; Orthodox Christians keep special Saturdays of the dead.
fact: Provody, also called Hrobky, is a Ukrainian day soon after Easter when families visit graves, bring Easter food and eggs, and remember their dead; eggs stand for new life and Christ's resurrection.
fact: Remembrance Day today remembers everyone who died in war, including civilians, as well as soldiers.
exercise: Taras's family hold sorokovyny forty days after Grandma died. Why the fortieth day? | In Orthodox tradition it is a special day to pray and remember | Because it takes forty days to plan a proper party for all the relatives | It is the day the will is read out | Because Grandma was forty years old
exercise: At sorokovyny they eat kutia. What is kutia made of? | Wheat, honey and poppy seeds | Rice, sugar, raisins and cinnamon | Potatoes, cabbage, onions and dill | Bread, cheese, butter and ham
exercise: What does the wheat in kutia stand for? | New life and resurrection | Hard work in the fields all summer | Wealth, money and a good harvest | The yellow colour of Ukraine's flag
exercise: Ellie's class makes paper poppies. Why poppies? | They grew on First World War battlefields where many died | They are the national flower of every single country in Europe | Soldiers ate poppy seeds for strength before a battle | Poppies are the only red flowers that bloom in November
exercise: Jake struggles to keep still for two minutes at 11 o'clock on 11 November. What is the silence for? | Respect for people who died in wars | A test to see who can stay quiet longest | Waiting for the broken school bell to be fixed by the caretaker | Listening for the sound of aeroplanes
exercise: Why is the silence at 11 o'clock on 11 November? | The First World War ended then, in 1918 | It was the King's birthday that year | The very first poppy was picked then | It is when schools start their morning break
exercise: Mila sees men from her village with the same surname on a memorial. What might that mean? | Some may have been brothers or relatives killed in the same war | Everyone in villages had to share one name | The names were copied twice by mistake | They were all famous generals
exercise: Liam goes to an Anzac Day dawn service with his grandad. Why so early? | It recalls the soldiers' landing at Gallipoli at dawn in 1915 | Because the service must finish before school | Because Australians always pray at dawn on every holiday of the year | Because the medals shine best at sunrise
exercise: Ellie's mum lights a candle every year on the day Granny died. Why? | The anniversary brings back strong feelings, and she remembers | She thinks Granny will come back that day | The church makes everyone do it | She is afraid of the dark that night
exercise: At All Souls' Day Mass, names of people who died that year are read aloud. Why? | It says each person mattered and is remembered | It checks who was a proper member of the church that year | It is to see who should get prizes | It warns people not to die next year
exercise: Vasyl's family leaves painted eggs on graves at Provody. Why eggs? | Eggs stand for new life and Christ's resurrection | The dead come out and eat them at night when nobody is looking | The eggs keep birds away from the graves | It is a race to see whose egg lasts longest
exercise: Ellie asks why Remembrance Day is only for soldiers. What is true? | It remembers everyone who died in war, civilians too | It is only for soldiers, because the law in Britain says so | It is only for soldiers who won medals for bravery | It is only for the generals in charge
exercise: What do Jews light on the anniversary of a death? | A yahrzeit candle | A big bonfire on the hill | A Christmas tree with lights | A firework at midnight
exercise: Which poem helped make the poppy a symbol of remembrance? | «In Flanders Fields» by John McCrae | «The Owl and the Pussy-cat» by Edward Lear | «Daffodils» by William Wordsworth | «Twinkle, twinkle, little star» by Jane Taylor
exercise: On which date is All Souls' Day? | 2 November | 25 December | 1 April | 14 February
test: Taras's English mate asks, «Why forty days? Why not just the funeral, innit?» What is the best answer? | In Orthodox tradition the fortieth day is a special time to pray and remember | It's just an excuse for another party | It is when the soul comes back home to eat | The funeral was too short, so they did it again
test: Taras's babusia (Ukrainian for «granny») says, «Eat your kutia, sonechko, it's for Grandma's memory.» («Sonechko» means «little sun», a pet name.) Why eat it? | Kutia is a traditional memorial dish, a sign of life and hope | It is the only food allowed after a death | Grandma's ghost wants to see it eaten | Kutia brings good luck at exams
test: Jake whispers, «Two minutes is ages, this is so long, bruh.» («Bruh» means «mate».) What is a good reply? | It's a short way to say thank you to people who gave so much | He's right, it's far too long and should be cancelled for good | Two minutes is too short to matter | It's just a game to see who talks first and loses
test: What would happen if nobody ever wrote names on war memorials? | Many real people who died would be forgotten | Wars would never have happened at all | Nobody would have died in any war | The names would appear on the stones by themselves
test: Liam's grandad, a proper Aussie, says, «Up at sparrow's fart for the dawn service, mate, too right.» («Sparrow's fart» is a joking phrase for «very early in the morning».) Why does he get up so early? | To remember soldiers who landed at Gallipoli at dawn | To catch the first bus to the beach before the crowds | Because the noisy sparrows wake him up | To beat the long queue at the shops
test: A Gen Z teen says, «Lighting a candle every year for your nan? Kinda extra.» («Extra» means «over the top».) What is fair? | Remembering on special days keeps love alive; it is normal | He's right, you should forget after a year | Candles bring the dead back, so it's risky | Only priests may light candles for the dead
test: At All Souls' Mass the priest says, formally, «Let us remember before God those who have departed this life in the past year.» What does «departed this life» mean? | Died | Moved abroad | Left the church | Gone on holiday
test: Vasyl's mate asks, «Why bring Easter eggs to dead people, are they hungry?» What is the true answer? | The eggs are a sign of new life and resurrection, a way of remembering | Yes, the dead eat them at night | It is to feed the cemetery birds | It is a prank on the gravediggers
test: A Gen Alpha boy says, «Remembrance is only for soldiers, the normal people don't count, no cap.» What is true? | It remembers everyone who died in war, civilians too | He is right, only soldiers count | It's only for soldiers who won their battles | It's only for the kings, queens and generals who died in wars
test: A New England grandpa says, «Wicked early, but we stand for the veterans every November, kid.» («Wicked» means «very»; «veterans» are people who served in wars.) What is he doing? | Joining a ceremony to honour people who served in wars | Getting up early to go fishing with his friends | Standing up at the back because there are no chairs left in the hall | Taking part in a running race
test: A cartoon shows a soldier on a poppy field getting up, dusting off and going home after the war. What is the truthful note? | Many soldiers did not come home; the poppy remembers them | Every soldier came home safely and lived to be very old | Poppies made wounded soldiers well again | Wars in the past had no deaths
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the observance of sorokovyny? | A memorial observance on the fortieth day following a death, comprising prayer and a shared meal | A celebration held forty days before a wedding | A festival marking the harvest of wheat | A ceremony for the reading of a will
exam: Identify the historical event commemorated by the silence observed at eleven o'clock on the eleventh day of November. | The armistice concluding the First World War in 1918 | The coronation of a monarch | The founding of the United Nations after the Second World War | The landing of troops at Gallipoli in 1915
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the scope of Remembrance Day as presently observed. | It commemorates all who died in war, civilians as well as members of the armed forces | It commemorates members of the armed forces exclusively, and no civilians whatsoever | It commemorates only those decorated for gallantry | It commemorates only those who died in the year preceding
exam: Ascertain the significance of the eggs deposited upon graves at Provody. | A symbol of new life and of the resurrection of Christ | Provision of nourishment for the deceased during the night | A means of deterring birds from the cemetery | A form of payment to the keepers of the cemetery
